<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pernicious anemia with B12 deficiency neuropathy, responding (D51.0)</content>. Reticulocytes have risen from 0.6 to 6.8 percent one week into treatment, which confirms the diagnosis and the response. Hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL and platelets 196 are already rising. Potassium 3.7 mmol/L is normal. The neurologic signs are unchanged, as expected this early. [S]<list><item>Weekly cyanocobalamin 1000 micrograms intramuscularly for four weeks, the first dose given today, then monthly for life.</item><item>Blood count and MMA at eight weeks; no repeat B12 level.</item><item>Upper endoscopy with gastric biopsies in about three weeks, as Dr. Salib advised.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic low back pain (M54.50)</content>. Exercise therapy under way. An NSAID is held until the platelet count is normal. [S]<list><item>Continue physical therapy weekly and the home program; heat for flares.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Reticulocyte count</td><td>One week after starting B12 replacement</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6.8 percent, up from 0.6: brisk response</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium during early replacement</td><td>In the first week of treatment of marked anemia</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3.7 mmol/L, normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count with MCV</td><td>With the one-week reticulocyte check, on hematology advice; again at eight weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL, MCV 110 fL, white cells 4.4, platelets 196: all improving</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>MMA</td><td>One week to three months after starting replacement</td><td>Due</td><td>At the eight-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Serum B12</td><td>Not routinely retested once replacement has begun</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not repeated</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Neurologic examination (vibration, proprioception, gait, cognition)</td><td>At diagnosis and at follow-up visits for B12 deficiency with neurologic findings</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Unchanged from intake: posterior column signs in the feet, Romberg positive; cognition normal</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Symptom assessment (fatigue, breathlessness, exercise tolerance)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tiredness and breathlessness improving</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Injection adherence</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven of seven daily doses given by the practice nurse; first weekly dose given today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Hematology</td><td>At diagnosis of pernicious anemia with cytopenias</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen this morning; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Upper endoscopy with topographic gastric biopsies</td><td>At diagnosis of pernicious anemia without recent endoscopy</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked in about three weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH for autoimmune thyroid disease</td><td>At diagnosis of autoimmune gastritis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>2.1 mIU/L last week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Iron studies</td><td>At diagnosis; recheck when red cell production recovers</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Ferritin 52 ng/mL and transferrin saturation 34 percent last week; recheck at eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Transfusion threshold</td><td>When hemoglobin is below 7 to 8 g/dL with symptoms</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL and rising; no cardiac symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise therapy for back pain</td><td>Weekly physical therapy for eight weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started five days ago; home program daily</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fall risk</td><td>Every visit while neuropathy persists</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Positive Romberg; night lights and a bedside lamp advised; no falls</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Normal hemoglobin and MCV within eight weeks of starting B12 replacement. In progress; target 1925-04-06 [S]</item><item>Stop the progression of neuropathy and recover balance, with no falls. In progress; target 1925-07-06 [S]</item><item>Reduce back pain to 3 of 10 or less and the Oswestry score below 20 percent by the five-month review. In progress; target 1925-07-06 [S]</item><item>Return to teaching full shooting classes. In progress; target 1925-07-06 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Vitamin B12 Deficiency: Recognition and Management. Am Fam Physician 2017;96(6):384-389 (2017); Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of cobalamin and folate disorders (2014); AGA Clinical Practice Update on the Diagnosis and Management of Atrophic Gastritis: Expert Review (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Weekly B12 injections, the first given today. Endoscopy in about three weeks. Review at eight weeks with blood count, MMA, iron studies, and back pain scores. Next scheduled visit 1925-04-06: eight-week pernicious anemia review, endoscopy results, and back pain review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
